#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "9377994d-f08b-57ee-8bd5-2315bf74a70f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21632" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b782a37c2e75edce01f6cc4204b8460eeeddfcda6c9a19e71d457f2d26c9e37.svg)\nSketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations above, given that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ![](images/e2/e2c67900a3ba263aad1f200c892be4d755ab441e6b96ad6317e9364121f597b4.svg) and ![](images/13/13ce986fa0d524d648d9ef04a684f3ea8f4a461551dc752a637421a892d6ab78.svg) for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ![](images/69/6909fb833286a13cf8c6c5bb40fde4090215e21536551aa1fa8ccac3b7d5ed65.svg) and ![](images/25/25ea6517999dfc2d7a84ff6ef0010ede0633c32b9673882d3ddf8535c4b140e8.svg) respectively." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/bc/bc65e04739f9a5ad34beeec6de1529e1ccecac090950e501d7eddda68b0a6c6d.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/ae/ae627e517d44488fe9878a28529140cc256eade4aadfe0445c8945ff066398ba.svg) has the equation \n![](images/39/39e03f3df5d6dd4a54b575a823498cda07cbe15e21e05a9cde5e0ad900def247.svg)\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/96/966a241524851dd38dc511f730fa023c3b2755f67a7fb1f6476ef4caf083ba94.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ![](images/de/def1e6afa80a61af78e69da17bdf65200185f760454a98ffa8b56142cd99b08f.svg) as ![](images/3a/3a4f4cfc9409e86d8918647d161f6cccccd1ba270e603575b8fc9f5167e58a48.svg) these solutions approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ff/ff535f17581c5a93045c3c23b8b33ca2996be6dfca63ed7808bc5fec809d2377.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/d5/d57858ae72e3b836de8c930917b65bcfef13cd3b85518933523024f84905a05b.svg) has the equation \n![](images/8f/8f96aa73ce843c7815a1fed6bf3f3774a173cfa4d56f13e1f3a8c48d53cd7097.svg)\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/b2/b2a991e2b459a17fc02d58d3eb267f04c03d03f8224561bc234513aa5dfc8a7a.svg)\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ![](images/b2/b2e3de236be1367c614d785fd065d91340a14b9197fb77734c537161645f69ba.svg) as ![](images/3a/3a4f4cfc9409e86d8918647d161f6cccccd1ba270e603575b8fc9f5167e58a48.svg) these solutions approach the origin as ![](images/e5/e54727d514ed264e6dd30c7f4044746e6d2b4b462dc3776daecf7eb68634c358.svg)\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/31/317fe507f591931ea371db43b78a83b1741c427ef89dd90571f0a0030ed70c20.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n![](images/87/878b11e2e3f3f3aaa6133482d483f1aa22ff420ade2a3d9533692f04c47348d1.svg)\nthus, ![](images/e2/e2c67900a3ba263aad1f200c892be4d755ab441e6b96ad6317e9364121f597b4.svg) is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ![](images/41/413cdb74711dd235ff727690ee5fb8713e6f50cbb49d131121a40b18b0943f53.svg) solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ![](images/ed/ed99403d5ac94993d00ca1f9c99def2e8d3bcbddba68d8aa1c1a8651fd140e2a.svg) as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/3a/3a6d8a97c6d07c78562c73dbd1abef1b1a3c0d9f38cd14f4f9874df091492b9e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166543 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "1c7567cf-f861-5088-b43e-ff94fcff0c91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21634" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=9x(t)+6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-6x(t)-6y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T,$ respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n![](images/9b/9b19dee8e197c29ab1802c7ea500f07185f24885702027a9726de39a91eacd9e.svg)\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/15/150fe59c158191129d89c43a1bb569e5c88b686c4365a6b06e661343f85b5e7d.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -\\dfrac12x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/f6/f6679165a59f48eb2944f4f8337ba990f2e82619e5e2a03308e0b4f663f9137c.svg)\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/e1/e1dcfabda7582534138b11b45a14cd8a8a9923c02d743ff99af08e487bcda254.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/3b/3ba48e81a2f6239f7cf0389017d823b87d558ded003eb6470ff6dc9078808b87.svg)\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point. \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/27/2710e11309547500aa3e9ba89f31281cbbc28244509046ed25a6c151c10c23ed.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166917 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "986f9190-9e73-5e08-b08d-ec779933b0cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21635" :question/problem "![](images/27/270b7c6ff796937603596f26e70886f41438ce87c043d3a1b4fc221398b8692f.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T,$ respectively. \n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1,$ $\\lambda_2,$ and $0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n![](images/0c/0c4b7d443c58e6720389306e73eb4e5e1934cfb7359221f36fb43ceb3e91e501.svg)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/32/3244f8128366a3f7f4ae3e41cd9323b1712f9587943f9fb2de54ee0c458feedf.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= -x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/d2/d241239c6475fbcf9559c2fcbbb8ca2805a6692fd1a9b3966fe8b38441075a4d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/c3/c3ac6997c0e8580f8d91b88578a02043ca495e99e3ea67bdf91b3eab9eb43d98.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_1 >  0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ![](images/77/77200c9fefd57e0b82b796072e07411f5fe10c91185d7d8f665f7ef6c3f54360.svg) has the equation \n\n$$\ny= 0,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb40117d63e76b2b2db59313b173b04a9a23ef2a28493a5041584e4c7c60c67d.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n![](images/0c/0ce3220f2cd013daba6b1f44f88d2834021b44ba877980f5ec47df7bc52396ce.svg)\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty.$ So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2.$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a9f3520-6f09-551d-9fa5-9f2f50c2e18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529715} {:answer/id #uuid "5afae28a-aff8-529b-8210-6a46b6913d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529799} {:answer/id #uuid "82f38ffa-d783-5749-96c3-dba5177e6ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529905} {:answer/id #uuid "ff09af8e-c840-58af-a731-12d11ef5b69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a9f3520-6f09-551d-9fa5-9f2f50c2e18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529715} :answer-field/id #uuid "c11806c3-3df1-5a93-a3c2-e8dfa9c8b462" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ebfbed-47b8-5b99-a11f-237d4b0fa3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529583} {:answer/id #uuid "2278409f-2918-5b5c-b25f-87796949067b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/va