 "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529607} {:answer/id #uuid "125c2c98-2434-5932-a480-2510a1b6f6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e536d67-208f-5109-860d-8ce8e8456549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529607} :answer-field/id #uuid "d09acdef-394b-56b7-aede-21eb94c695dd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21116522-1e68-5aeb-8229-3d0039b396bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-333649" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=2x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9108600b-92c8-5cac-8349-d5b082ccbc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529932} {:answer/id #uuid "ea06fb62-1823-5c87-b12d-1aaa69446262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9108600b-92c8-5cac-8349-d5b082ccbc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529932} :answer-field/id #uuid "067d8b08-6895-5be5-a9f6-a2b1e3667c90" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cac2988-a080-595f-843f-97eb5958cbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529602} {:answer/id #uu